  <!-- SECTION 3: FREQUENTLY ASKED QUESTIONS -->
  <div class="content-card">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Markup</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["What is the difference between markup and profit margin?", "Markup is based on the COST of the item, while profit margin is based on the SELLING PRICE of the item. For example, if you buy an item for $50 and sell it for $100, your profit is $50. Your markup is 100% ($50 ÷ $50), but your profit margin is 50% ($50 ÷ $100). Profit margin can never exceed 100%, whereas markup can be infinite."], ["How do I calculate a 50% profit margin?", "To achieve a 50% profit margin, you must apply a 100% markup to your cost. This is known as \"keystone pricing\" in retail. You take the wholesale cost of the item and double it. If an item costs $50, you sell it for $100. Your $50 profit is exactly half (50%) of your $100 selling price."], ["What is a typical retail markup percentage?", "Standard retail markups typically range between 50% (33.3% profit margin) and 100% (50% profit margin). High-turnover grocery products operate on 15% to 20% markups, electronics range from 25% to 40%, apparel averages 100% to 150%, and jewelry or cosmetics can reach 300% to 500%+ to offset inventory holding costs."], ["How do you calculate markup percentage from cost and price?", "The markup formula is: Markup % = ((Selling Price − Cost) ÷ Cost) × 100. First, find dollar gross profit by subtracting wholesale cost from selling price. Then divide gross profit by cost and multiply by 100."], ["Can markup percentage exceed 100%?", "Yes, absolutely. A markup can technically be infinite. If a restaurant buys ingredients for a fountain beverage for $0.20 and sells the cup for $2.00, the gross profit is $1.80. The markup is ($1.80 ÷ $0.20) × 100 = 900%, while the profit margin is ($1.80 ÷ $2.00) × 100 = 90%."], ["Why do retailers use markup instead of margin?", "Retailers use markup because it allows quick mental or invoice-level pricing. If a shop operates on a flat 50% markup rule, staff simply multiply wholesale invoice prices by 1.50 to immediately determine price tags."], ["How do I find cost if I know the selling price and markup?", "To calculate original wholesale cost, use: Cost = Selling Price ÷ (1 + (Markup % ÷ 100)). For example, if an item sells for $150 and has a 50% markup: $150 ÷ 1.50 = $100 wholesale cost."], ["Should freight and shipping be included in cost?", "Yes. Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) must incorporate wholesale purchase cost, inbound freight, customs duties, and direct prep packaging. Omitting inbound freight results in artificially inflated markup and inaccurate financial statements."], ["What is the formula to convert margin to markup?", "To convert profit margin into required markup percentage, use: Markup % = (Profit Margin % ÷ (100% − Profit Margin %)) × 100. For example, to achieve a 40% margin: 40 ÷ (100 − 40) = 40 ÷ 60 = 66.7% markup."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
    <div class="faq-box">
      <div class="faq-question"><%= f[0] %></div>
      <div class="faq-answer"><%= f[1] %></div>
    </div>
    <% }); %>
  </div>

